ns, R-052): each project's workflow state and its runs since the previous evaluation — first `gh workflow list --all`, where any disabled state (`disabled_inactivity`, or `disabled_manually`, hidden the same way and started by no `gh workflow run`) is the third kind below whatever the runs show, since `gh run list --workflow dependency-scan.yml` lists no run of a disabled workflow without `-a` (gh's own reference, read 2026-11-01 on gh 2.97.0: `gh workflow list` "hiding disabled workflows by default", and both commands' `-a, --all  Include disabled workflows`) and an old success would otherwise read green; then `gh run list --workflow dependency-scan.yml --created ">=<the previous evaluation's date>"`, the date the ledger (`Reports/ledger.json`) keeps as its `_meta.last_extract` stamp for the previous run, read before this run's extract overwrites it (a nothing-new week leaves no run folder, so the folder is not the record), listed with `--json databaseId,createdAt,status,conclusion`, the window being the listed runs whose `createdAt` is later than the `last run read:` time the previous evaluation's Dependency scan section gives for the project, the creation time of the newest completed run that evaluation read, or the previous section's value where its window completed none, `none` where there was no previous one (a section reading `none` leaves every listed run in the window, and where no previous section names the project the window is the listed runs created on a later day than that date, so a run of that day cannot cover this Sunday: Review A and Review B, TASK-041, round 2): the date is a day and the schedule runs on the morning of the evaluation's own Sunday, so the date alone would take in the run already read and hide a late or missing schedule behind it; a creation time, not the run's id, since GitHub documents no order of its ids; a run re-run from the Actions tab keeps its creation time and is not read again, the rerun the evaluation names (`gh workflow run`) starting a new run; and the section carries `finding standing: run <id>` from week to week until a later run ends `success`, so a finding the narrower window would drop is reported every week it stands (Review A and Review B, TASK-041); a project whose workflow list holds no `dependency-scan.yml` is `not configured`, its calibration's item, with `last run read: none`; the window gives each run its status and conclusion, and for a `failure` `gh run view <run id> --log-failed` gives the step's `dependency-scan: OSV-Scanner exit <code>` line, which tells the first two kinds apart — in **three kinds, defined here once** (R-052, Failure modes and the inventory's row say the same): (1) **a finding**: a completed run of the window whose conclusion is `failure` and whose exit line reads `exit 1` — a flaw, fixed by a `DEP-<nnn>` increment, the merge policy's procedure, never by an unreviewed change; a finding stands until a later run ends `success`, in that window or a later one, a later run of the second kind superseding nothing, both then reported; (2) **a scanner failure**: the window's last completed run proved nothing — a `failure` with any other exit code (127, 128 or another) or with no exit line at all (the step never reached the scanner: the checkout or the download failed), or any conclusion other than `success` and `failure` (`cancelled`, `timed_out`, `startup_failure`, `skipped`, `action_required`, `neutral`, `stale`) — reported as such with the rerun's one command, `gh workflow run dependency-scan.yml`, the rerun read at the next evaluation; (3) **no completed run since the previous evaluation**: the workflow disabled, no run of the window listed, a list the login cannot read, or only runs still queued or in progress at the reading (a late schedule) — a finding of its own, never green (KP-005: silence is not green), reported with its remedy: the enabling run sheet below where the workflow is disabled, the rerun command where no run is listed, and none where a run is in progress, which the next Sunday reads. A window whose last completed run ended `success` with no finding standing is green. The host fact that makes the third kind certain, read from GitHub's documentation on 2026-11-01 ("Disabling and enabling a workflow"): *in a public repository, scheduled workflows are automatically disabled when no repository activity has occurred in 60 days*; a schedule may also start late. So every paused or finished public project reaches the third kind within two months, the evaluation names it, and the remedy is a run sheet for that project's owner — `gh workflow enable dependency-scan.yml`, then `gh workflow run dependency-scan.yml` (or the Actions tab's *Enable workflow*) — the enabled run read at the next evaluation; the documentation names public repositories, and a private one is read the same way, its missing run the same finding. Two more of GitHub's facts, read from the same documentation ("Events that trigger workflows"): *this event will only trigger a workflow run if the workflow file exists on the default branch* (`workflow_dispatch`), and *scheduled workflows will only run on the default branch*; so the workflow's first proof, one `workflow_dispatch` run read green, comes after the file reaches the default branch — after the configuration increment's merge for a project, after this repository's increment merges here — read by the supervisor then and by the evaluation at the latest, never a Done criterion before the merge. Dependabot alerts: the owner switched them on (2026-11-01, "Yes, I switched Dependabot on"); whether GitHub's separate option that enables them for new repositories was taken is not verified, so the evaluation reads each project in two calls that tell the three results apart: `gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/vulnerability-alerts` answers 204 where the alerts are enabled and 404 where they are not (GitHub's REST documentation, read 2026-11-01; the owner's login has the admin read it needs on their ow